  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.5 x^2 + z^2) / 2, drawn at 0.4 times the loss. It is steeper in x, so overshoot starts there: x zig-zags above learning rate 0.4 and grows (diverges) above 0.8.",
    "Noise is Gaussian around the true slope with spread 1.5*sqrt((64-B)/(63B)) per setting for batch size B. That is the sampling spread from drawing B of 64 points, and it is exactly zero at 64.",
    "Both arrows show steps (learning rate times slope), drawn at 2 times their true length. The orange one is the move the ball makes next. The grey one is the step the full-data slope would give.",
    "A run ends after 40 updates (one every 6 host ticks), or as diverged when the loss passes 5 or the ball leaves the drawn bowl. The trail is clipped to the bowl edge on screen.",
    "Noise comes from a fixed seed. Reset replays the same sequence, and each new run uses the next values."
  ]
}